<article class="env-remark" id="009C">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/009C">Remark <span data-tag="009C">2.2.6.5</span>.</a> Let $F: \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}\rightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{D}}$ be a functor of bicategories. Then $F$ is an isomorphism (in the sense of Definition <a data-tag="0098" href="/tag/0098">2.2.6.1</a>) if and only if it satisfies the following conditions: </p>
<p><ul>
<li><p>
The functor $F$ induces a bijection from the set of objects of $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ to the set of objects of $\operatorname{\mathcal{D}}$. 
</p>
</li><li><p>
For every pair of objects $X,Y \in \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$, the functor $F$ induces an isomorphism of categories $\underline{\operatorname{Hom}}_{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}(X,Y) \rightarrow \underline{\operatorname{Hom}}_{\operatorname{\mathcal{D}}}( F(X), F(Y) )$. 
</p>
</li>
</ul></p>
</article>
